Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; Milwaukee working-class grit into Seattle gray. PETER - lean young man, dark hair, quiet eyes, pizza-shop clothes, later a settled man in a simple coat. NICK - taller, reddish-brown messy hair, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, glue-gun puzzle face, steel presence. MA - older woman, tired brown eyes, thinning brown hair, house dresses. SIONNA - pale English-story skin, short-to-shoulder brown hair, light blue dawn eyes, simple thrift clothes, fish necklace.

FADE IN:

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Gray mist hangs over Belltown. PETER, forties, lean in a plain coat, walks past a keyboard player in a felt hat. A pizza storefront reads TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

(SOUND: wet tires, distant ferry horn, soft keyboard jazz)

PETER (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not the stupid book. My real Big Brother. Nick.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A fifth-floor walk-up. Brick walls, dented furniture. Young PETER, early twenties, hauls grocery bags. NICK, scarred chin and left eyebrow, reddish-brown hair wild, carries six bags like a strongman. MA sits half-asleep in a brown chair before a game show.

(SOUND: TV game-show bells, floorboards creak, bags rustle)

PETER (V.O.)
We lived downtown on Kirk Street after Dad took off. Nick had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls.

NICK drops bags on the counter. Ma doesn't look up.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleepin' in the middle of the day.

PETER
I will. I promise.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY

Kids play kickball with dented garbage-can lids for bases. A ball smashes through a kitchen window. Glass rains. LITTLE MARK freezes. Young NICK, twelve, steps forward as MRS. ENGEL storms out.

(SOUND: glass shatter, alley echo, distant traffic)

NICK
I did it. It was me.

PETER (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was Mark. Somehow a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks for the window. I was sure proud of him.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Weights clang. A lifter mid-preacher-curl sees NICK enter and drops a forty-pound bar. Nick nods, takes a fifty for warm-ups. PETER watches from the side, smaller, awed.

(SOUND: iron plates slam, grunts, chain clinks)

PETER (V.O.)
When he walked in the bar or the gym, people moved. A real-life Viking. Modern-day warrior. Milwaukee. Always Milwaukee.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

PETER locks a bike. SIONNA, pale, short brown hair, light blue eyes, black V-neck, fish necklace, backpack, trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: bike chain tick, street noise, soft stumble)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

He barely hears. Her eyes hold him.

PETER
Want to come in for pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes a number. Walks away, glances back over her shoulder.

(SOUND: footsteps recede, door chime as Peter enters work)

PETER (V.O.)
No girl had ever been interested in me. It was like I knew her. Intense. Like waves at the ocean covering my feet and I didn't want to move.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA lines pill bottles, glassy-eyed, mumbling.

(SOUND: bottles clink, fridge hum)

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She stares through him, then snaps back.

MA
Oh. Right. I am so sorry.

PETER (V.O.)
I would have to tell Nick. He'll get mad like it was my fault.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers-Bears on TV. NICK slaps PETER's back. Pool balls crack in the next room.

(SOUND: crowd roar on TV, bar chatter, cue ball break)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

PETER
Ma was all confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. Do something before we pay for a nursing home. I don't have the money for that shit.

PETER
I'll watch her. Walk tomorrow.

Nick's look could punch. Peter pays and leaves into cold air.

EXT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - NIGHT

PETER hurries up stone steps under old lights.

(SOUND: wind, door heavy open, quiet hush inside)

INT. LIBRARY - NIGHT

Wood floors, chain-hung lamps. PETER at the desk with a temporary paper card, then in classic literature stacks. He pulls Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: pages flutter, stamp thump, distant chair scrape)

LIBRARIAN
Plastic card in the mail within the week.

PETER (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything was free in this building?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma asleep. PETER reads in bed lamplight. Heathcliff and Catherine. He drifts.

(SOUND: page turn, radiator tick, soft snore)

PETER (V.O.)
Somehow in my dream I mixed Sionna and Catherine. Old clothes. Horses. I would end up feeling for her just like Heathcliff.

EXT. DREAM FIELD / FOREST - DAY

Stylized: PETER in white sleeves and red tunic helps SIONNA in linen down from a dappled mare. Light blue eyes. First kiss in long grass.

(MUSIC: low ethereal strings)

(SOUND: horse breath, wind in leaves, distant birds)

They embrace. He bolts upright in real bed, sweating.

INT. PETER'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

PETER gasps upright, chest pounding.

(SOUND: bedsprings, his hard breath, cold room silence)

PETER
Hold— What the hell was that?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, steam. SIONNA at a back table in green hooded top. PETER sits. Her eyes stop time.

(SOUND: espresso hiss, low indie music, cup set down)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks ghosts, doubles, moors. Wild love that never changes destroys.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

PETER (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake. We made another date. Friday. Dinner.

EXT. DOWNTOWN STREET - DAY

They walk. PETER processes.

(SOUND: bus air-brake, footsteps)

PETER (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left and she never moved on. There ain't no way I could ever forgive him. Nick became my dad when I was eight.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. PETER in t-shirt and shorts struggles through poses, shirt falling over his face until he tucks it. SIONNA flows easy. Final savasana: stillness.

(SOUND: instructor's calm voice, collective breath, soft music drone)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
I felt so dumb. But after... I wouldn't care if someone called me a big pussy. Just calm.

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. That's why they call it practice.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

NICK stomps. MA in her chair. PETER enters.

(SOUND: door slam potential, Nick's boots, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I forgot. I was with someone. A girl. A couple dates.

NICK
A girl. That's worth all this? Look at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out.

(SOUND: door slam, echo in hall)

PETER (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? He is so off the handle. She has herself together.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Months later. Loud rock. NICK, BOB, SAMMY drunk-loud. PETER apart. LINDSEY appears with a dark-haired boyfriend. Nick's face goes red-fire.

(SOUND: AC/DC on speakers, pool crack, rising voices)

NICK
Who's that asshole?

PETER
You broke up with her. Leave it.

Nick shoves past. Faces off with the boyfriend. Crowd stills.

BOYFRIEND
You got a problem? You're in my face like a little bitch!

PETER steps between, closer than ever to raging Nick.

PETER
Nick, listen. You don't know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're next if you don't get out of my face.

Nick's fist draws back toward Peter's face in slow motion. JOE and a BOUNCER rush in. Chaos. Peter runs into the night.

(SOUND: scuffle, head-butt thud, running feet on pavement, heartbeat)

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

PETER bursts in, shaking. SIONNA holds him on a green suede sofa.

(SOUND: night wind outside, his ragged breath, soft house creak)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares about you does that.

PETER
I love Nick. He's my brother. He always took care of us.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don't let him stop your purpose.

PETER
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it.

He sleeps with his head in her lap. Dream: angel Sionna from water; giant Nick with a mace; she pushes him back with light.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Time passed. Pill organizer on the table. SIONNA cooks. MA laughs at old farm stories. NICK enters in boots and suede jacket. First meeting.

(SOUND: fork on plate, TV cop show, awkward silence)

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. Nice to meet you. I've heard so much about you.

NICK
Uh, yea. Likewise.

He refuses pie. Sneers at university talk. She answers with calm stats on women in college. Nick stunned, then thin snake smile.

NICK
Someone in this room has a job tomorrow. Take care of this numbskull.

He whispers at the door to Peter.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Later. Empty. Blinking answering machine. PETER plays messages.

(SOUND: machine beep, officer voice, hospital nurse voice)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, Milwaukee County Sheriff. We need to talk regarding your family.

NURSE (V.O.)
Patricia Owens, Frodo Hospital, update on your family members.

Peter's face goes white. He bikes hard.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Stone 1898 building. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, sits PETER down with water.

(SOUND: station murmur, phone ring distant, styrofoam cup shake)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Your brother Nicholas and your mother Olivia. Hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

Peter cannot hold the cup steady.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology pending. They think he may have been drunk.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU HALL - DAY

Through glass: NICK's wild hair, tubes, machines. PETER looks once.

(SOUND: monitor beep, cart wheels, muffled PA)

PETER
No. I'm really not up to seeing him.

He walks out.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Empty brown chair. Afghan. PETER collapses crying, screaming into space.

(SOUND: raw sobs, fist on floor, empty room ring)

PETER
Die you fucking jackass. Die.

SIONNA enters, holds him.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it all out.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket. Few mourners. Dirt hits maple lid.

(SOUND: muted thunk of dirt, quiet sobs, wind in trees)

PETER (V.O.)
I was the only son there. Rightly so. He killed her.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Days later. NICK bruised yellow-green, chest tube, leg huge under sheet. Weak voice.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, IV drip, muted TV off)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears. He talks Jack and Cokes before church. Vehicular manslaughter talk. Lawyer. Disability.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

PETER
Think about it.

Peter turns and leaves. Nick trapped in the bed.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Months of strain. Then: knock. NICK forces in. SIONNA alone. Gray cat flees. Hippie sandals, dish rag.

(SOUND: hard knock, door chain, cat scatter, kettle far off)

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You messed with my brother. School. Crazy ideas. He would jump off a bridge if you asked.

SIONNA
Let's talk rationally. This has nothing to do with me.

He backs her to the wall. Hand on her neck. Switchblade flicks open. Blade at collarbone.

(SOUND: switchblade snick, her choked squeak, his heavy breath)

NICK
Break it off tonight. Cash in a week. Leak one word and I have friends. Parents too. Understand?

Door SLAMS open. PETER.

PETER
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Blade hits. Red. Sionna sinks. Nick elbows Peter, wipes blade on her shirt, runs.

(SOUND: body thud, wet gasp, running feet, Peter’s scream)

PETER
Stay with me— stay—

He rips cloth, stacks hands on the wound, blood everywhere, dials 911 with red fingers.

(SOUND: sirens approach, his counting breath, her shallow air)

EXT. HOSPITAL / TIME PASSAGE - NIGHT

Sirens. Gurney. Later: SIONNA in recovery, foam pillow, blank ocean eyes. PETER beside her through weeks.

(SOUND: monitor beep steady, hallway hush)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

February light. SIONNA in the rocking chair, hem of shirt in fingers. PETER on the couch.

(SOUND: chair rock creak, winter quiet)

PETER
You know I love you, right? I feel like you're blaming me.

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe a break. Mom and Dad's. Therapist.

PETER
Stay. Please.

She walks to the bedroom. He leaves for work early. Free fall.

PETER (V.O.)
She was my life guide. She woke me. And then she was gone.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Years later. PETER walks mist again. Tony's Boy's Pizza. Dogs, languages, gray blanket sky.

(SOUND: ferry horn, keyboard, rain mist on hood)

PETER (V.O.)
Seven years. Chain of pizzerias. Hatred for my brother like a fly on the picnic watermelon. Then a dream. A sage. Stop judging. The pizza box is illusion. Forgiveness.

INT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY

Barista CHRIS, blonde messy bun, hoop earrings, smiles. PETER finds courage.

(SOUND: grinder, milk steam, soft chatter)

PETER
I'm Peter. Want to see that play on the poster?

CHRIS
Okay. Yeah.

He almost smiles full.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Green Bay. Plexiglass. Phones. NICK enters older, graying, thinner, bags under sad eyes. They stare.

(SOUND: door buzz locks, muffled voices, phone plastic click)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
Not a cakewalk. Parole maybe in two.

PETER
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen. Even though you are my brother, I am no longer interested in seeing you. You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

Nick's eyes shine.

PETER
And this is the most important bit. I forgive you. I forgive you for being a huge asshole because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

NICK
I'm so sorry. You're so right. I am a huge asshole. You're more than I ever was. You deserve to live out there.

GUARD (O.S.)
Time's almost up.

PETER
After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I love you.

Nick is led away. Peter hangs up, light.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT - DAY

PETER drives rolling green hills, then floats through airport throng. Boards. Looks at the pass.

(SOUND: tires on wet road, airport PA, jet whine)

PETER (V.O.)
The weight of thirty years gone. Somewhere she's happy. I send Nick love and know I will never see him again by choice.

He whispers to himself, almost smiling.

PETER
That's me. President Peter Olson.

(MUSIC: quiet hopeful piano under ferry-horn memory)

PETER (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
